course: everyday
lesson: 113
title: Babies, button batteries and magnets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A baby's body is still weak in places: its neck cannot hold up its heavy head, and its brain can be badly hurt by shaking, so babies are handled only gently and only with a grown-up's say-so.
explain: Some small things in a home are far more dangerous than they look: a coin-sized button battery and a pair of tiny strong magnets can do terrible harm inside a child's body.
explain: A swallowed button battery makes a current in the wet throat and can burn through it within about two hours, and two swallowed magnets can pull together through the walls of the gut, the long tube food passes through, and tear it.
explain: For example, a boy in Florida sees his baby sister holding the little round battery from the TV remote; he takes it away, tells his mum at once, and she checks whether any is missing.
explain: Batteries also need care when they are old, leaking or swollen, because their chemicals burn skin and big lithium batteries can catch fire.
fact: A baby should be picked up only when a grown-up says so and shows you how to support its head.
fact: A baby must never be shaken, because shaking can cause serious brain damage.
fact: Babies try to eat creams and wipes, so nappy cream, wipes and lotion are kept out of their reach.
fact: Cords, strings and long scarves are kept away from small children because they can wrap around the neck.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat within about two hours, so the child must go to hospital immediately.
fact: If a child swallows two small strong magnets, they can pull together through the gut and tear it, so medical help is needed at once.
fact: Batteries should be recycled at a battery bin, because in ordinary rubbish they can leak poison and start fires in bin lorries.
fact: The white crust on a leaking battery can burn skin, so it should not be touched with bare hands.
fact: A phone or tablet battery that swells or gets very hot can catch fire, so it should be unplugged and put on a hard surface away from things that burn.
fact: Strong magnets can wipe the strips on bank cards and damage some electronics.
exercise: A baby is crying near you and no grown-up is in the room. What should you do? | Call a grown-up rather than pick the baby up alone | Pick the baby up quickly under its arms and walk around with it | Rock the pram as hard as you can | Shake the baby gently until it stops
exercise: Why must a baby's head be supported when it is held? | Its neck is too weak to hold up its head | Its head is lighter than its body | Babies like to have their soft hair touched when they are held | The head keeps the baby warm
exercise: Why must a baby never be shaken, even to stop crying? | Shaking can cause serious brain damage | Shaking makes babies cry for longer only | Shaking makes babies hungry and grumpy later | Shaking is fine if it is gentle and quick
exercise: Why are nappy cream and wipes kept away from a baby's hands? | Babies try to eat creams and wipes | Babies are scared of their strong smell | Cream goes off if a baby touches it | Wipes dry out if a baby holds them too long
exercise: A long ribbon is tied to your toddler cousin's toy. What should you do? | Take it off, because cords can wrap around a small neck | Make it longer so he can drag the toy | Tie it around his wrist so that he can keep the toy close all day long | Leave it because ribbons are soft
exercise: A toddler may have swallowed a button battery. What must happen? | Go to hospital immediately, without waiting for signs | Wait to see if it comes out tomorrow | Give the toddler a fizzy drink | Make the toddler run and jump around to shake the battery loose again
exercise: Why is a swallowed button battery so dangerous? | It can burn through the throat within about two hours | It is far too big to pass through a small child's body | It makes the child very sleepy only | It explodes as soon as it is swallowed
exercise: Your little brother swallowed two small magnets from a toy. Why is that an emergency? | They can pull together through the gut and tear it | Magnets make children too heavy | Magnets stop the heart from beating | They will make his teeth go magnetic and stick together
exercise: Where should old batteries go? | To a battery recycling bin in a shop or recycling centre | Into the kitchen rubbish bin with all the other things | Into the garden compost heap to rot down | Down the toilet
exercise: Why are batteries dangerous in an ordinary bin? | They can leak poison and start fires in bin lorries | They make the rubbish bags far too heavy for anyone to lift | They attract rats and mice | They charge up other rubbish
exercise: You open the torch and the battery has white crusty stuff on it. What do you do? | Do not touch it with bare hands and let a grown-up clean it | Scrape it off with your fingernail | Lick your finger and wipe it clean | Shake the torch hard over the carpet until the crust falls off
exercise: Your tablet is swollen and very hot while charging. What is right? | Unplug it, put it on a hard surface away from anything that can burn and tell a grown-up | Keep playing on it until the battery runs flat, then it will cool down by itself | Put it under your pillow so the soft cotton can cool it down slowly | Squeeze it flat again with your hands
exercise: Why should a strong magnet not be left on top of a bank card? | It can wipe the card's magnetic strip | It makes the card heavier and harder to bend | The card can slowly pull the magnet apart | It changes the numbers printed on the card
test: Which of these is the most dangerous for a toddler to swallow? | A button battery | A small piece of banana | A sip of warm milk | A soft crumb of bread
test: A girl in Brisbane thinks her baby brother put a button battery in his mouth, but he seems fine. What should happen? | He goes to hospital straight away, because the burn starts before you can see it | Wait until he shows that he is ill, because most swallowed things come out on their own | Give him a big glass of water and forget about it, as it washes the battery down | Look in his mouth tomorrow
test: Why is one swallowed magnet worrying but two magnets much worse? | Two can pinch the gut between them and tear it | Two magnets weigh far too much for a child to swallow | One magnet always comes back up | Two magnets make a loud noise inside
test: Why is a babysitter told never to shake a crying baby? | A baby's brain can be badly hurt by shaking | Shaking makes the baby forget its parents | Shaking only works to calm older babies, not newborns | It makes the baby too tired to eat
test: A boy wants to carry his newborn cousin across the room. What should happen first? | A grown-up agrees and shows him how to support the head | He should lift her up quickly by her arms so she does not slip | He should practise by swinging her gently | He should wait until she is crying
test: A family keeps old batteries in a jar and takes them to the supermarket battery bin. Why is this good? | It keeps poison out of the ground and fires out of bin lorries | It makes the old batteries charge up again so that they can be used once more | It is the only way to buy new batteries | Supermarkets pay a lot of money for them
test: Your phone gets hot and the back starts to bulge. What could happen if you ignore it? | The battery could catch fire | The phone would get faster at loading | The bulge would go down by itself after a sleep | The screen would just get brighter
test: Why are long cords on blinds tied up high in a home with a toddler? | Cords can wrap around a small child's neck | Toddlers pull the blinds right off the windows and break them | Cords collect too much dust | Tied cords make the room darker
test: Mum's handbag is open on the floor with face cream inside. Why is that a worry with a baby nearby? | The baby may eat the cream | The baby will be allergic to the bag | The cream will melt on the floor | The baby will lose the bag somewhere
test: think the baby swallowed one of those little round batteries!! | Go to hospital right now, it can burn the throat in hours | Give her milk and wait a day | It will just come out the other end in a day, don't worry about it | Only worry if she starts coughing
test: baby wont stop crying, can i give him a lil shake | No, never shake a baby, get a grown-up | Yes, gently shaking calms babies down | Yes, if you hold his head | Only for a few seconds
test: the old remote battery has white crusty stuff on it | Don't touch it bare-handed, it can burn skin | Lick it to see if the battery still works | Scrape it off with your fingers | It's just salt, it's harmless
test: my phone battery's all puffed up and hot | Unplug it and put it on a hard surface away from things that burn | Keep charging it so it calms down and goes back to normal | Put it under your pillow to cool | Press it flat again
explain: A toddler in Texas swallowed a button battery from a TV remote; within hours it burnt the inside of his throat, and he needed emergency surgery.
explain: A girl in the UK swallowed two small magnets; they joined through her gut and made holes in it, and she needed an operation.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat in about two hours, so it needs emergency help straight away.
fact: Swallowing two or more small magnets can tear holes in the gut and needs urgent help.
test: think baby swallowed a coin battery!! | Tell an adult and call the emergency number now | Wait to see if it comes out by itself tomorrow | Give her a drink and wait | Make her jump to shake it out
test: my mate swallowed two of those little magnet balls lol | Tell an adult now, they can hurt his gut | It's fine, they always come out the other end | Give him more to push them | Tell him to lie down and rest
test: remote's battery cover broke, battery's loose | Tape it shut or put it away from little ones | Leave it, kids won't touch a remote anyway | Give it to the baby to hold | Put the battery on the floor
test: my cousin said a kid swallowed a remote battery but "it's fine, they just poop it out". true? | No, it can burn the throat in hours, go to hospital now | Yes, just wait for it to come out the other end in a day or two | Yes, a glass of milk makes it pass safely | Yes, unless the battery was a brand-new one
test: did u hear a girl swallowed 2 magnets n they "stuck her whole belly together like glue". what's probs exaggerated | The whole belly bit; they can join through the gut and tear it | That swallowed magnets need medical help at once | That two swallowed magnets can pull together through the walls of the gut | That she needed an operation after it
test: everyone's saying the toddler next door swallowed a battery cos his mum left the remote out, pass it on? | No, do not spread it; just keep batteries away from little ones | Yes, the neighbours have a right to know exactly what happened to the toddler | Yes, but only tell it to the grown-ups | Yes, as long as you do not name the mum
exam: Ascertain, in respect of a button battery that has been swallowed, the approximate period within which it may burn through the throat. | About two hours | About two whole weeks | About two full days | About two months
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard occasioned by the ingestion of two small strong magnets? | They may attract through the gut walls and tear them | They are wholly harmless and pass out unaltered | They are dangerous solely if they are rusty | They may interfere with the electricity of the stomach and stop it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of spent batteries. | Recycling at a battery bin, as rubbish bins risk leaks and fires | Ordinary household rubbish, since spent batteries are inert | Burial in the garden, as the soil neutralises all of the chemicals | The kitchen sink, where water dilutes any remaining charge
exam: Whereas a tablet battery is observed to swell or become very hot, determine the action to be taken. | Unplug it and place it on a hard surface away from fuel | Continue charging it so that the swelling subsides | Wrap it in a blanket to contain the excess heat | Press firmly on the swelling to flatten the casing
